(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-physiology-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form C","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Mammotropes secrete prolactin, whose primary function is to:","choices":["Stimulate milk production and secretion","Stimulate spermatogenesis","Stimulate body growth","Stimulate glucocorticoid secretion from the adrenal cortex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Prolactin, secreted by mammotropes, stimulates milk production and secretion. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Cortisol is produced in which zone of the adrenal cortex?","choices":["Adrenal medulla","Zona fasciculata","Zona reticularis","Zona glomerulosa"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cortisol is produced in the zona fasciculata, the middle zone of the adrenal cortex. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Estrogen's effect on vaginal epithelium is to change it from:","choices":["Cuboidal to stratified","Simple squamous to transitional","Stratified to cuboidal","No change in epithelial type"],"answer":0,"exp":"Estrogen changes the vaginal epithelium from cuboidal to stratified. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"The trigone of the bladder is best described as:","choices":["A small triangular area immediately superior to the bladder neck, bounded by the ureteral openings and internal urethral orifice","The dome-shaped, most distensible portion of the bladder that expands superiorly as urine gradually fills it","The peritoneal fold of visceral fascia that suspends and anchors the bladder to the pelvic side wall","The muscular ring of dense skeletal fibers that forms and controls the external urethral sphincter"],"answer":0,"exp":"The trigone is a small, smooth triangular area immediately superior to the bladder neck, with the two ureteral orifices at its upper corners and the internal urethral orifice at its lower point. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Type A intercalated cells are responsible for which acid-base function?","choices":["Na+ reabsorption exclusively","ADH-dependent water reabsorption","HCO3- secretion alone, with no H+ secretion","H+ secretion and HCO3- reabsorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Type A intercalated cells secrete H+ and reabsorb HCO3-, generating new bicarbonate in the process. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Calcitonin, secreted by thyroid C-cells during hypercalcemia, lowers blood calcium primarily by:","choices":["Inhibiting osteoclasts and thereby reducing bone resorption","Increasing intestinal absorption of dietary calcium","Activating myosin light chain kinase within bone cells","Directly stimulating osteoclasts to accelerate bone resorption"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: calcitonin acts mainly by inhibiting osteoclasts, reducing bone resorption and lowering blood calcium. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 64"},{"q":"Where does growth hormone primarily act, per this lecture?","choices":["The thyroid gland exclusively","The pancreas and spleen exclusively","The liver and peripheral tissues","The kidney and adrenal cortex exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Growth hormone acts on the liver and peripheral tissues. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"In the summary of glucose regulation, what is insulin's role?","choices":["Only affects glucose in the kidney","Increased by glucose; returns glucose to normal","Has no relationship to plasma glucose","Increased by low glucose; raises glucose toward normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Insulin is increased by glucose and acts to return glucose to normal (i.e., lower elevated glucose). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Implantation of the fertilized ovum into the endometrium is driven primarily by which cells?","choices":["Trophoblast cells","Sertoli cells","Leydig cells","Parathyroid chief cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Trophoblast cells invade the endometrial lining, envelope the blastocyst, provide nutrients, and later develop into the placenta and membranes of pregnancy. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which nerve carries skeletal motor fibers that allow voluntary control over the external bladder sphincter?","choices":["Vagus nerve","Pelvic nerve","Pudendal nerve","Hypogastric nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pudendal nerve carries skeletal motor fibers innervating the external bladder sphincter, giving voluntary/somatic control over urination. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which equation correctly relates excretion to the basic mechanisms of urine formation?","choices":["Excretion = Secretion − Filtration − Reabsorption","Excretion = Filtration − Reabsorption + Secretion","Excretion = Reabsorption − Filtration + Secretion","Excretion = Filtration + Reabsorption − Secretion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Excretion = Filtration − Reabsorption + Secretion — what's filtered, minus what's reabsorbed back into blood, plus what's actively secreted into the tubule. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Relaxation of smooth muscle is produced when:","choices":["Calcium binds to calmodulin to form an active complex","A second molecule of adenosine triphosphate is hydrolyzed","Myosin light chain kinase phosphorylates the regulatory light chain","Myosin light chain phosphatase dephosphorylates the light chain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: dephosphorylation of the myosin light chain by the phosphatase causes relaxation. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 44"},{"q":"What effect does dopamine, functioning as prolactin-inhibiting hormone, have on lactotropes?","choices":["Inhibits prolactin secretion","Stimulates prolactin secretion","Converts lactotropes into somatotropes","Has no effect on lactotropes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Dopamine, acting as prolactin-inhibiting hormone, inhibits prolactin secretion by lactotropes. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Somatostatin's paracrine action within the pancreatic islets is to:","choices":["Have no effect on insulin or glucagon","Stimulate only insulin secretion","Stimulate both insulin and glucagon secretion","Inhibit both insulin and glucagon secretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Somatostatin acts as a paracrine inhibitor of both insulin and glucagon secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Besides maternal oxytocin, parturition also involves fetal release of oxytocin, cortisol, and:","choices":["Growth hormone only","Insulin","Prostaglandins","Aldosterone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Fetal release of oxytocin, cortisol, and prostaglandins all contribute to the parturition process. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"The glomerular filtration barrier is composed of which three layers?","choices":["Endothelium, mesangium, and juxtaglomerular cells","Fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocytes","Mesangium, basement membrane, and tubular epithelium","Endothelium, smooth muscle layers, and adventitial connective tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"The filtration barrier consists of fenestrated endothelial cells, the basement membrane, and epithelial podocytes surrounding the outer surface of the basement membrane. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Full RAAS activation causes vasoconstriction of which arterioles specifically?","choices":["Efferent arterioles","Neither — RAAS only affects venous tone","Both afferent and efferent equally","Afferent arterioles"],"answer":0,"exp":"RAAS activation results in vasoconstriction of the efferent arterioles specifically, as part of the cascade's overall sequence of effects. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Type I collagen secreted by osteoblasts constitutes approximately what fraction of the protein in bone?","choices":["70%","100%","90%","45%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: type I collagen makes up about 90% of the protein in bone. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 51"},{"q":"Where in the hypothalamus is oxytocin primarily produced?","choices":["Supraoptic nuclei exclusively","Arcuate nucleus","Ventromedial nucleus","Paraventricular nucleus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Oxytocin is produced in the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the overall physiological purpose of somatostatin's inhibitory actions, per this lecture?","choices":["To permanently shut down pancreatic hormone secretion","To maximize nutrient loss through the GI tract","To stimulate rapid, unregulated glucose absorption","To maximize use of available fuel and inhibit growth hormone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Somatostatin's purpose is described as maximizing the use of available 'fuel' and inhibiting growth hormone. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"True labor has a circadian rhythm, with contractions peaking during which time window?","choices":["Midnight to 5 am","6 am to 9 am","There is no time-of-day pattern","Noon to 3 pm"],"answer":0,"exp":"True labor has a circadian rhythm, peaking between midnight and 5 am. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Why do glomerular capillaries filter fluid at a much higher rate than most other capillaries in the body?","choices":["They have unusually low hydrostatic pressure and an unusually small Kf value","They are the only capillary beds in the body containing fenestrations","They have high glomerular hydrostatic pressure and a large capillary filtration coefficient","They completely lack any filtration barrier separating blood from filtrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"The combination of a high glomerular hydrostatic pressure and a large Kf gives glomerular capillaries a much higher filtration rate than typical capillaries elsewhere in the body. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Granular (JG) cells of the JGA are best described as:","choices":["Podocyte-derived cells specialized to filter plasma proteins","Modified smooth muscle cells that sense perfusion pressure and release renin","Modified epithelial cells that primarily secrete protective mucus","Specialized neurons belonging to the enteric nervous system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Granular (JG) cells are modified smooth muscle cells that release renin, and (as part of the broader JGA response) contribute to sensing perfusion. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which substance is a PARACRINE mediator that relaxes vascular smooth muscle?","choices":["Cholecystokinin","Nitric oxide","Antidiuretic hormone","Angiotensin II"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: nitric oxide is a paracrine mediator that relaxes blood vessels. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 46"},{"q":"The 'somatomedin hypothesis' relates growth hormone's growth-promoting effects to which mediator?","choices":["Thyroid hormone (T3/T4) acting directly","Insulin, acting directly on its own receptor","Cortisol, a glucocorticoid stress hormone","IGF-1 (insulin-like growth factor 1)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The somatomedin hypothesis proposes that many of GH's growth-promoting effects are mediated through IGF-1. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Cortisol enhances fatty acid oxidation as part of its fat metabolism effects. What is the moderate quantitative effect on free fatty acids (FFA) for energy utilization?","choices":["FFA availability for energy decreases substantially","FFA availability for energy increases moderately","FFA are completely eliminated from circulation","FFA levels are entirely unaffected by cortisol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cortisol moderately increases FFA availability for energy utilization, mobilizing fatty acids as part of its overall fat-metabolism effects. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Menarche is defined as:","choices":["The permanent cessation of menstrual cycles, with average onset near age 51","The onset of male puberty, typically occurring around age 12","The point at which estrogen levels are said to permanently peak for life","The beginning of the cycle of menstruation, with average onset before age 13"],"answer":3,"exp":"Menarche is the beginning of the cycle of menstruation, with an average age of onset under 13 years. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"The internal urethral sphincter is formed by:","choices":["The pudendal nerve's motor endplate region within the urogenital diaphragm","A purely mucosal fold of tissue with no underlying muscular component at all","Detrusor muscle interlaced with a large amount of elastic tissue at the bladder neck","Voluntary skeletal muscle fibers located at the urogenital triangle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The internal sphincter is detrusor muscle interlaced with substantial elastic tissue within the bladder neck. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Angiotensinogen, the precursor in the RAAS cascade, is produced by which organ?","choices":["Lung","Kidney","Adrenal cortex","Liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Angiotensinogen is produced in the liver. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Compared with skeletal muscle, smooth muscle can shorten over a much larger range, achieving approximately what percentage of shortening?","choices":["85-95%","60-75%","10-20%","30-45%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: smooth muscle can operate over a large length range, with 60-75% shortening possible. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which receptor location would a water-soluble peptide hormone most likely act on, given it cannot easily cross the lipid cell membrane?","choices":["It has no receptor interaction","Cell cytoplasm receptors","Cell nucleus receptors","Cell membrane receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Water-soluble hormones (like peptides) generally act on cell membrane receptors, since they cannot easily diffuse across the lipid bilayer to reach intracellular receptors. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which of the following increases aldosterone secretion, per this lecture's control summary?","choices":["ACTH acting alone as the single primary rate-limiting regulator","Increased extracellular fluid (ECF) volume acting alone","Decreased renin-angiotensin system (RAS) activity alone","Increased potassium and increased renin-angiotensin system (RAS) activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Increased potassium and increased RAS activity are the most important regulators that increase aldosterone secretion; ACTH is needed for secretion but does not itself set the rate. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why must the testes be kept about 2°C cooler than core body temperature?","choices":["Sperm actually require elevated core body heat to mature properly and would rapidly die if kept at lower, cooler temperatures.","Sperm are fragile, survive only a matter of days at body temperature, and have limited energy capacity, making cooler temperatures protective","There is no meaningful temperature sensitivity at all in sperm production, maturation, or long-term survival.","Cooler scrotal temperature directly increases Leydig cell testosterone production and overall secretion rate."],"answer":1,"exp":"Sperm are fragile with limited energy capacity and only survive a matter of days at body temperature, so keeping the testes cooler than the core helps protect them. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which clinical-adjacent but still physiology-based reason is given for why GFR matters to assess?","choices":["It is unrelated to overall kidney function and instead reflects only systemic vascular resistance","It reflects how well the kidneys are filtering blood and whether kidney function is normal, decreased, or severely impaired","It only matters for calculating cardiac output and has no relevance to assessing renal function","It has no practical significance and is purely an academic calculation with no clinical bearing"],"answer":1,"exp":"GFR tells us how well the kidneys are filtering blood and whether kidney function is normal, decreased, or severely impaired, and helps monitor kidney function over time. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"As tubular fluid travels down the descending limb and then up the ascending limb, its osmolality:","choices":["Becomes more concentrated going down, then less concentrated going up","Becomes less concentrated going down, then more concentrated going up","Stays constant throughout","Only changes in the ascending limb, never the descending limb"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fluid becomes more concentrated as it travels down the descending limb (iso-osmotic going in, increasingly concentrated) and less concentrated (hypo-osmotic) as it travels up the ascending limb. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"When the acetylcholine (ACh)-gated cation channel at the motor end plate opens, what is the net ion movement?","choices":["Net inward movement of chloride ions that hyperpolarizes the fiber","Equal cation flux producing no depolarization","Net outward movement of potassium ions that repolarizes the membrane","Net inward movement of positive ions, mostly sodium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the channel is nonselective among sodium, potassium, and calcium, but the net movement is inward, mainly sodium. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 11"},{"q":"In GH-driven bone growth, chondrocyte precursor cells are converted into which cell type that GH also stimulates?","choices":["Myocytes","Adipocytes","Osteoblasts","Osteoclasts"],"answer":2,"exp":"GH increases conversion of chondrocytic cells to osteogenic cells and stimulates osteoblasts, driving bone growth. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Aldosterone's action in the kidney occurs at which nephron segment, abbreviated 'DT' in the lecture's figure?","choices":["Bowman's capsule","Loop of Henle","Distal tubule","Proximal tubule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Aldosterone acts at the distal tubule (DT) and collecting duct. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Progesterone's effect on the breasts includes promoting the secretory capability of which structures?","choices":["Lobules and alveoli","The nipple-areolar complex exclusively","Ductile system growth only","Adipose tissue deposition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Progesterone promotes development of lobules and alveoli and their secretory capability, while estrogen contributes more to stromal tissue, ductile system growth, and fat deposition. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which best summarizes the composition of normal glomerular filtrate?","choices":["Rich in both cellular elements and protein content, similar in composition to whole blood plasma.","Composed almost entirely of plasma protein, with no salts or organic molecules present at all.","Rich in blood cells but completely free of protein, salts, and organic molecules like glucose.","Essentially free of cells and containing only tiny amounts of protein, but including salts and organic molecules like glucose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal glomerular filtrate has no cellular elements and only tiny amounts of protein, but does contain salts and organic molecules such as glucose. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which nephron segment's key physiologic role is described simply as 'water removal'?","choices":["Descending limb","Proximal convoluted tubule","Late distal convoluted tubule","Ascending limb"],"answer":0,"exp":"The descending limb's key physiologic role is water removal (high water reabsorption, low NaCl reabsorption). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which pair correctly matches each hormone with its effect on blood calcium?","choices":["Both parathyroid hormone and calcitonin lower blood calcium","Parathyroid hormone lowers calcium; calcitonin raises calcium","Both parathyroid hormone and calcitonin raise blood calcium","Parathyroid hormone raises calcium; calcitonin lowers calcium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: parathyroid hormone raises blood calcium while calcitonin lowers it. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 59"},{"q":"Why does T3 act faster than T4 despite T4 being secreted in greater quantity?","choices":["T4 cannot bind the receptor at all","T3 has a higher affinity for the thyroid hormone nuclear receptor and a shorter latency","T3 has a longer half-life than T4","T3 is not bound to any plasma protein while T4 is fully protein-bound, explaining all the speed difference"],"answer":1,"exp":"T3 has a higher affinity for the thyroid hormone receptor than T4 and a shorter latency (6–12 hours) compared to T4's 2–3 day latency. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"ACTH increases cortisol production partly by increasing which cellular process, besides increasing P450scc?","choices":["Increasing lipoprotein uptake into the cell and transport of cholesterol to mitochondria","Decreasing cholesterol availability entirely by suppressing hepatic LDL receptor expression","Increasing renal sodium excretion through a direct action on renal tubular cells","Decreasing lipoprotein uptake into the cell while increasing cholesterol efflux"],"answer":0,"exp":"ACTH increases lipoprotein uptake into the cell and increases transport of cholesterol to the mitochondria, supplying substrate for steroidogenesis alongside increasing P450scc. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Estrogen's skeletal effect involves osteoclastic activity regulated via which mechanism?","choices":["Complete suppression of all bone remodeling","Direct stimulation of osteoclast proliferation with no inhibition","An osteoclastogenic inhibitory factor","No skeletal effect at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Estrogen's skeletal effect on osteoclastic activity operates through an osteoclastogenic inhibitory factor. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What happens if efferent arterioles constrict too severely?","choices":["Renal blood flow, filtration fraction, and glomerular colloid osmotic pressure all decrease","Renal blood flow decreases while filtration fraction and glomerular colloid osmotic pressure increase","GFR increases indefinitely as efferent resistance continues rising unchecked","Renal blood flow increases sharply while filtration fraction holds steady"],"answer":1,"exp":"Severe efferent arteriolar constriction decreases renal blood flow, while filtration fraction and glomerular colloid osmotic pressure both increase as the efferent arteriole over-constricts. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which of the following decreases aldosterone secretion?","choices":["Elevated angiotensin II activity","Increased plasma potassium (K+) levels","Elevated circulating ACTH hormone levels","Increased Na+ concentration (osmolality)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Increased Na+ concentration (osmolality), along with atrial natriuretic factor (ANF), decreases aldosterone secretion. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"The F-actin thin filament is a double-stranded helix of polymerized G-actin. What is bound at each G-actin active site?","choices":["An ADP molecule","A troponin C subunit","A calcium ion","A tropomyosin strand"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: each G-actin carries a bound ADP molecule at its active site, where myosin heads attach. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 22"},{"q":"What happens to mean arterial pressure and pulse pressure under increased thyroid hormone?","choices":["Both mean pressure and pulse pressure increase substantially","Mean pressure decreases while pulse pressure is unchanged","Mean pressure is largely unchanged, while pulse pressure increases","Both mean arterial pressure and pulse pressure decrease substantially"],"answer":2,"exp":"Under increased thyroid hormone, mean arterial pressure is largely unchanged, while pulse pressure increases, alongside increased cardiac output, tissue blood flow, and heart rate. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Structurally, insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin are all classified as which hormone type?","choices":["Tyrosine derivatives","Steroid hormones","Peptide hormones","Eicosanoids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin are all peptide hormones, water soluble. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) regulates spermatogenesis by stimulating which cells?","choices":["Trophoblast cells","Sertoli cells","Leydig cells","Chief cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"FSH stimulates Sertoli cells, which provide support for spermatogonia. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"When GFR increases and more NaCl reaches the macula densa, what is the immediate downstream effect on the afferent arteriole?","choices":["No effect occurs at all, since the macula densa only responds to low NaCl delivery","Renin release increases sharply, which then dilates the afferent arteriole","The efferent arteriole, rather than the afferent arteriole, constricts sharply in direct response","Adenosine and ATP are released, causing afferent arteriole constriction, and GFR decreases toward normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Increased NaCl delivery to the macula densa triggers release of adenosine and ATP, which constrict the afferent arteriole and bring GFR back down toward normal. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What determines transport in the distal convoluted tubule, per this lecture's nephron segment overview?","choices":["The medulla's osmolality","Hormone presence exclusively, with no osmotic contribution","The osmolality of the cortex","Blood pressure alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"The osmolality of the cortex determines transport in the distal convoluted tubule. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"The synaptic cleft of the neuromuscular junction is 20-30 nm wide and contains large quantities of which enzyme?","choices":["Myosin light chain kinase (MLCK)","Acetylcholinesterase (AChE)","Lysyl hydroxylase","Alkaline phosphatase (ALP)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the synaptic cleft contains large amounts of acetylcholinesterase, which degrades acetylcholine. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary cell type produces adrenocorticotropin (ACTH)?","choices":["Corticotropes","Somatotropes","Lactotropes","Gonadotropes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Corticotropes produce ACTH (adrenocorticotropin/corticotropin). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Where is somatostatin produced within the pancreas?","choices":["D cells, pancreatic islets","α cells, pancreatic islets","β cells, pancreatic islets","Acinar cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Somatostatin is produced by D cells of the pancreatic islets (also produced elsewhere, e.g. the hypothalamus). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Per the composition of semen described in this lecture, what are the approximate volume contributions of sperm, prostate secretions, and seminal vesicle secretions?","choices":["33% each","90% sperm, 5% prostate, 5% seminal vesicles","10% sperm, 30% prostate, 60% seminal vesicles","50% sperm, 25% prostate, 25% seminal vesicles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Semen is composed of approximately 10% sperm, 30% prostate secretions, and 60% seminal vesicle secretions, with an average pH of 7.5. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which structures can modulate (inhibit or facilitate) the micturition reflex?","choices":["The parathyroid gland tissue","The cerebral cortex and brainstem","The pancreatic islet cells","The adrenal medulla only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The micturition reflex, while a spinal reflex, can be inhibited or facilitated by centers in the cerebral cortex and brainstem. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What determines transport across the collecting duct, according to this lecture's nephron segment overview?","choices":["It transports only solutes and never permits any water transport","It is always freely permeable to water, hormone-independent","Water can be transported across only in the presence of certain hormones","Transport there is determined solely by cortical osmolality"],"answer":2,"exp":"Water can be transported across the collecting duct only in the presence of certain hormones (i.e., it is hormone-dependent, primarily via ADH). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Occasional spontaneous fusion of a single acetylcholine (ACh)-filled vesicle with the presynaptic membrane produces which event?","choices":["Calcium-induced calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","A propagated muscle action potential that spreads across the fiber","A miniature end plate potential that does not cause a contraction","A sustained tetanic contraction of the entire muscle fiber"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: single-vesicle release produces a miniature end plate potential, which does not trigger contraction. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 8"}]};
